1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,010
Every day, a Red Army soldier
did dozens of jobs to stay alive.

2
00:00:04,880 --> 00:00:07,670
He dug. He marched. He
wrapped his feet in rags.

3
00:00:07,880 --> 00:00:12,190
But one tiny job, ten seconds with a
pencil, many of them refused to do.

4
00:00:13,180 --> 00:00:16,230
Decades later, people still
dig in forests because of it.

5
00:00:16,820 --> 00:00:18,850
So let's live one ordinary day with him.

6
00:00:19,540 --> 00:00:21,050
Not a battle. Just a day.

7
00:00:21,180 --> 00:00:23,070
Because most of his war was not shooting.

8
00:00:23,840 --> 00:00:25,230
It was everything in between.

9
00:00:25,640 --> 00:00:29,270
Four in the morning, somewhere on the
Eastern Front, nineteen forty-three.

10
00:00:30,460 --> 00:00:32,420
Our soldier wakes up in a zemlyanka.

11
00:00:32,440 --> 00:00:36,610
That is a dugout. A hole in the
ground, roofed with logs and earth.

12
00:00:36,840 --> 00:00:39,130
In winter, a small stove fights the cold.

13
00:00:40,040 --> 00:00:41,950
In spring, the floor turns to mud.

14
00:00:42,280 --> 00:00:45,090
Several men sleep here, shoulder
to shoulder, in their coats.

15
00:00:46,100 --> 00:00:48,610
But who is he? Not simply a Russian.

16
00:00:48,780 --> 00:00:51,870
The Red Army drew men from
across the whole Soviet Union.

17
00:00:52,400 --> 00:00:56,270
He could be Ukrainian, Kazakh,
Georgian, Belarusian or Russian.

18
00:00:57,160 --> 00:01:00,150
Some soldiers were women.
Hundreds of thousands of them.

19
00:01:00,800 --> 00:01:02,690
His first job of the day is his feet.

20
00:01:03,400 --> 00:01:06,380
He has no socks. Instead,
he has portyanki.

21
00:01:06,400 --> 00:01:10,870
Two rectangles of cloth, wrapped tightly
around each foot before the boot goes on.

22
00:01:11,820 --> 00:01:14,620
Sounds miserable. But
there was a logic to it.

23
00:01:14,640 --> 00:01:18,900
A wet sock stays wet. A foot wrap
can be unwound, turned around,

24
00:01:18,920 --> 00:01:21,230
and wrapped again with
the dry end on the toes.

25
00:01:21,860 --> 00:01:24,370
For a man who walked
everywhere, that mattered.

26
00:01:24,580 --> 00:01:26,350
The idea lasted a long time.

27
00:01:27,100 --> 00:01:31,330
The Russian army only started replacing
foot wraps with socks in twenty thirteen.

28
00:01:32,380 --> 00:01:33,870
But dry feet will not feed him.

29
00:01:34,560 --> 00:01:36,170
And breakfast had a strange problem.

30
00:01:37,020 --> 00:01:39,350
Breakfast came in the dark. Why?

31
00:01:39,440 --> 00:01:41,230
Because a field kitchen makes smoke.

32
00:01:41,380 --> 00:01:45,410
And smoke on the horizon tells the
enemy's artillery exactly where to aim.

33
00:01:46,280 --> 00:01:49,020
So the kitchens cooked
before dawn or after sunset,

34
00:01:49,040 --> 00:01:51,250
a few kilometers behind the line.

35
00:01:52,160 --> 00:01:54,370
The food could not come
to the soldiers on wheels.

36
00:01:54,540 --> 00:01:55,830
So men carried it.

37
00:01:56,380 --> 00:02:00,210
They hauled pots and thermoses forward
through the trenches, under fire,

38
00:02:00,240 --> 00:02:03,770
often at night. Some of them
never came back with the soup.

39
00:02:04,460 --> 00:02:07,510
In the ideal plan, a soldier
got hot food twice a day.

40
00:02:08,380 --> 00:02:10,330
Once before dawn. Once after dark.

41
00:02:10,500 --> 00:02:13,290
In practice, front-line
men often ate once a day.

42
00:02:14,060 --> 00:02:17,230
The rest was dry bread, crackers,
and whatever was in a tin.

43
00:02:17,740 --> 00:02:20,890
So he has eaten. The sun is coming up.

44
00:02:20,960 --> 00:02:23,950
What does a soldier do with a
whole day when nobody is attacking?

45
00:02:24,980 --> 00:02:28,250
He digs. Every time a
unit stopped, the men dug.

46
00:02:28,420 --> 00:02:30,650
A hole to lie in. A trench to move in.

47
00:02:31,320 --> 00:02:34,910
A shelter for the night. His
small spade was not a spare tool.

48
00:02:35,600 --> 00:02:39,370
It was the one thing between him
and every shell that landed nearby.

49
00:02:40,300 --> 00:02:42,530
Look at Kursk, in the summer
of nineteen forty-three.

50
00:02:43,280 --> 00:02:44,530
Before the battle,

51
00:02:44,620 --> 00:02:48,860
soldiers and civilian workers dug
defensive lines on a scale that is hard to

52
00:02:48,880 --> 00:02:49,880
imagine.

53
00:02:50,580 --> 00:02:54,610
Thousands of kilometers of trenches,
in belt after belt, across one region.

54
00:02:55,400 --> 00:03:00,230
That was not done by machines. It was done
by people like him, one spade at a time.

55
00:03:00,920 --> 00:03:02,190
But a trench does not move.

56
00:03:02,820 --> 00:03:07,440
And the Red Army was about to spend
two years moving west. First, though,

57
00:03:07,460 --> 00:03:10,830
every soldier had to survive a
season that no trench could stop.

58
00:03:11,300 --> 00:03:13,730
But there was a problem
the spade could not solve.

59
00:03:14,560 --> 00:03:18,050
Winter. On the Eastern Front, the
cold could kill as surely as a shell.

60
00:03:18,760 --> 00:03:20,370
So his day changed with the season.

61
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:22,730
His greatcoat was not just a coat.

62
00:03:23,420 --> 00:03:24,670
At night, it was his blanket.

63
00:03:25,520 --> 00:03:29,060
In winter, many soldiers wore a
quilted jacket called a telogreika,

64
00:03:29,080 --> 00:03:32,840
and boots made of pressed
felt, called valenki.

65
00:03:32,860 --> 00:03:36,310
In the first winter of the war,
outside Moscow, that gear mattered.

66
00:03:37,240 --> 00:03:40,310
Many German units had arrived
without proper winter clothing.

67
00:03:40,960 --> 00:03:44,330
Soviet units fighting there were
often better dressed for the cold.

68
00:03:45,080 --> 00:03:46,730
But warm clothes do not carry shells.

69
00:03:47,640 --> 00:03:50,150
And as the front moved,
the real question became:

70
00:03:50,440 --> 00:03:52,950
how do you supply an army
that is always walking?

71
00:03:53,860 --> 00:03:58,690
So he walks. Most of the Red Army's
infantry crossed Eastern Europe on foot.

72
00:03:59,360 --> 00:04:03,230
With a rifle, a rolled greatcoat,
a gas mask bag, and that spade.

73
00:04:03,900 --> 00:04:04,900
But here is the twist.

74
00:04:04,920 --> 00:04:08,900
The trucks that rolled past him,
carrying his shells and his bread,

75
00:04:08,920 --> 00:04:12,110
were often American. Through Lend-Lease,

76
00:04:12,140 --> 00:04:16,450
the United States sent the Soviet Union
hundreds of thousands of trucks and jeeps.

77
00:04:17,400 --> 00:04:18,990
The most famous was the Studebaker.

78
00:04:19,820 --> 00:04:21,270
He even ate American food.

79
00:04:22,380 --> 00:04:24,410
Soldiers got tins of American canned meat.

80
00:04:24,500 --> 00:04:26,290
And they gave it a sarcastic nickname.

81
00:04:27,140 --> 00:04:28,400
They called it the second front.

82
00:04:28,420 --> 00:04:30,210
Because for years,

83
00:04:30,320 --> 00:04:33,820
they had been waiting for the Allies
to open a real second front in Western

84
00:04:33,840 --> 00:04:37,990
Europe. That front only opened
in June nineteen forty-four.

85
00:04:38,420 --> 00:04:40,390
The tins arrived long before it.

86
00:04:40,980 --> 00:04:42,530
So his body is moving and fed.

87
00:04:43,300 --> 00:04:45,370
But what kept a man going, day after day?

88
00:04:45,980 --> 00:04:47,930
For many, the answer came in a small cup.

89
00:04:48,720 --> 00:04:51,070
On the twenty-second of
August, nineteen forty-one,

90
00:04:51,420 --> 00:04:55,820
the State Defense Committee signed
decree number five hundred sixty-two.

91
00:04:55,840 --> 00:05:00,290
It gave front-line troops one hundred
grams of forty-percent vodka a day.

92
00:05:01,420 --> 00:05:03,990
Soldiers called it the People's
Commissar's hundred grams.

93
00:05:04,280 --> 00:05:05,630
The idea was not new.

94
00:05:06,600 --> 00:05:09,490
In January nineteen forty,
during the war with Finland,

95
00:05:09,640 --> 00:05:11,560
a similar ration had been proposed.

96
00:05:11,580 --> 00:05:16,550
One hundred grams of vodka and fifty
grams of pork fat, against the cold.

97
00:05:17,300 --> 00:05:20,850
But there was a catch. The
ration was not for everyone.

98
00:05:21,080 --> 00:05:23,530
It was meant for the men in
the first line of battle.

99
00:05:24,020 --> 00:05:26,930
And over the next two years,
the rules kept changing.

100
00:05:27,760 --> 00:05:30,510
By nineteen forty-three, the
daily mass issue was stopped.

101
00:05:31,320 --> 00:05:34,230
The hundred grams were kept
mainly for units on the attack.

102
00:05:35,160 --> 00:05:38,130
Think about what that meant. If
the cup came round in the morning,

103
00:05:38,220 --> 00:05:40,890
it could mean the day would
not be ordinary at all.

104
00:05:41,820 --> 00:05:43,370
But the evening still had to come.

105
00:05:44,020 --> 00:05:45,650
And the evening had its own rituals.

106
00:05:46,380 --> 00:05:50,600
In the evening, he smokes. He gets
coarse tobacco called makhorka.

107
00:05:50,620 --> 00:05:55,650
Cigarette paper is scarce. So he rolls
it in whatever paper he can find.

108
00:05:55,940 --> 00:05:57,990
Very often, that was a scrap of newspaper.

109
00:05:59,160 --> 00:06:02,250
Then he writes home. He
takes one sheet of paper.

110
00:06:02,400 --> 00:06:03,930
No envelope. No stamp.

111
00:06:04,160 --> 00:06:07,450
He folds it into a triangle, and
writes the address on the outside.

112
00:06:08,720 --> 00:06:09,890
Mail from the front was free.

113
00:06:10,540 --> 00:06:14,550
A military censor read it, and blacked
out anything that could help the enemy.

114
00:06:15,400 --> 00:06:19,590
For a family back home, a triangle in
the post was the best news in the world.

115
00:06:20,040 --> 00:06:22,510
It meant he had been
alive when he wrote it.

116
00:06:22,900 --> 00:06:24,210
Families kept them for decades.

117
00:06:25,260 --> 00:06:28,610
Thousands of these triangles
now sit in museums and archives,

118
00:06:28,740 --> 00:06:31,270
still folded the way
a soldier folded them.

119
00:06:32,140 --> 00:06:35,290
So every week, he wrote his own
name on the outside of a letter.

120
00:06:36,160 --> 00:06:39,990
Remember that. There is one more
thing to understand about his day.

121
00:06:40,820 --> 00:06:44,430
He could not leave it. Front-line
soldiers almost never got home leave.

122
00:06:45,120 --> 00:06:47,390
A political officer
watched the unit's mood.

123
00:06:47,880 --> 00:06:52,080
In July nineteen forty-two, Order number
two hundred twenty-seven declared:

124
00:06:52,100 --> 00:06:54,330
not one step back.

125
00:06:54,940 --> 00:06:57,290
Retreating without orders
could be treated as treason.

126
00:06:58,300 --> 00:07:00,760
Men who broke the rules
could be sent to penal units,

127
00:07:00,780 --> 00:07:02,910
and given the most dangerous tasks.

128
00:07:03,560 --> 00:07:05,010
So his day had no exit.

129
00:07:05,680 --> 00:07:08,190
There was only the next
day, and the one after that.

130
00:07:08,240 --> 00:07:12,290
Which brings us to night. And
to the one job he refused to do.

131
00:07:12,720 --> 00:07:15,830
Around his neck, or in a small
pocket, he carried a little capsule.

132
00:07:16,460 --> 00:07:19,350
Black, about five centimeters
long, with a screw-on lid.

133
00:07:20,200 --> 00:07:23,010
The Red Army introduced it
in March nineteen forty-one.

134
00:07:24,220 --> 00:07:25,250
Inside was a slip of paper.

135
00:07:26,060 --> 00:07:28,980
On it, he was supposed to write
his name, the year he was born,

136
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:33,830
and his home address. Ten seconds
with a pencil. That was the job.

137
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:37,850
He also had a small Red Army book,
a kind of paybook with his details.

138
00:07:38,880 --> 00:07:41,590
But books burned, soaked
through, or were taken.

139
00:07:41,880 --> 00:07:45,010
The capsule was meant to survive
when everything else did not.

140
00:07:45,680 --> 00:07:48,070
And many soldiers simply never did it.

141
00:07:48,720 --> 00:07:52,630
Search volunteers today say that most
capsules they find were never filled in.

142
00:07:52,980 --> 00:07:55,620
Why? Superstition.

143
00:07:55,640 --> 00:07:59,590
Many men believed that filling in the
slip was like marking yourself for death.

144
00:08:00,460 --> 00:08:02,910
Some kept a needle and thread
in the capsule instead.

145
00:08:03,760 --> 00:08:08,230
Think about the contrast. This man wrote
his name on a letter home every week.

146
00:08:08,860 --> 00:08:12,500
But he would not write it on the one
piece of paper meant for the day he died.

147
00:08:12,520 --> 00:08:15,150
Look at every other job in his day.

148
00:08:15,980 --> 00:08:17,590
The foot wraps. The soup. The digging.

149
00:08:17,640 --> 00:08:20,090
The walking. Each one was
a way of reaching tomorrow.

150
00:08:21,180 --> 00:08:24,830
Writing that slip was the only job
that admitted tomorrow might not come.

151
00:08:25,640 --> 00:08:27,750
The cost of that choice
is still being counted.

152
00:08:28,600 --> 00:08:32,830
Millions of Soviet soldiers are
still listed as missing. Every year,

153
00:08:32,860 --> 00:08:36,640
volunteer search teams walk into the
forests and fields of the old Eastern

154
00:08:36,660 --> 00:08:39,810
Front. They find helmets, buttons, boots.

155
00:08:40,380 --> 00:08:43,470
Sometimes they find a capsule.
They open it carefully.

156
00:08:43,700 --> 00:08:45,690
And very often, there is no name inside.

157
00:08:46,620 --> 00:08:49,150
Without a name, a soldier
cannot be sent home.

158
00:08:49,400 --> 00:08:52,430
He is reburied with others,
under a stone that says unknown.

159
00:08:53,500 --> 00:08:57,010
His family may have waited their whole
lives for a letter that never came.

160
00:08:57,940 --> 00:09:00,330
So what did a Red Army
soldier refuse to do?

161
00:09:00,960 --> 00:09:02,320
Everything else, he did.

162
00:09:02,340 --> 00:09:04,670
He refused to write the
end of his own story.

163
00:09:05,440 --> 00:09:08,270
But what did he eat when the
field kitchen never came at all?

164
00:09:08,680 --> 00:09:10,190
That is the next story.

165
00:09:10,300 --> 00:09:11,730
Subscribe, so it finds you.
